{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0125VZMY6Ro9di2BiMyRvwHD","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (41.3KB). Full output saved to: /ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M87PSMWSQ6G6WD0VA3GY9E2E/8c6e0074-f595-4004-ac32-dfbe34f1c171/tool-results/btyied08c.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\npackage closeout\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/github\"\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/gitx\"\n)\n\nconst (\n\tmaxInlineRunes   = 2000\n\tmaxBulletRunes   = 300\n\tmaxReviewRunes   = 60000\n\treviewFooterRoom = 2000 // runes kept free for the closing lines\n)\n\n// Review modes: how the review reached GitHub.\nconst (\n\tReviewInline  = \"inline\"  // one PR review with inline comments\n\tReviewBody    = \"body\"    // a PR review with every finding in its body\n\tReviewComment = \"comment\" // a plain PR comment (GitHub took no review)\n\tReviewFound   = \"found\"   // an earlier post was found; nothing was posted\n)\n\n// ReviewContext is what the reviewer loop knows that the role file cannot say.\n// Added is used by Prepare only and is not persisted: the plan keeps the\n// decided placement.\ntype ReviewContext struct {\n\t// MergeBase is the commit the diff was taken against.\n\tMergeBase string\n\t// Added is, per path, the head-side line ranges the PR added.\n\tAdded map[string][]gitx.LineRange `json:\"-\"`\n\t// HeadMoved is set when the PR gained code (anything outside .ard/) after\n\t// the commit the review pinned. An approve is then not a pass: the review\n\t// is posted but no label says done, so the request stays and runs again.\n\tHeadMoved bool\n}\n\n// ReviewPlan is the PR review close-out posts for a review job that reached a\n// verdict.\ntype ReviewPlan struct {\n\tCommitSHA string // the pinned commit the agent reviewed\n\tVerdict   string // after any downgrade\n\t// Downgraded: the role file said approve but carried a blocking finding.\n\tDowngraded bool\n\tHeadMoved  bool\n\tInline     []Finding // on lines the PR added: posted as inline comments\n\tOther      []Finding // everything else: listed in the review body\n\t// NoLines: the added lines were not known (a Reaper close-out after a\n\t// restart), so every finding is in Other.\n\tNoLines bool\n\t// PRGone is set (merged, closed or branch gone) when the PR was gone at\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0e3acb37-8020-42c2-ba96-d30c407bec32","uuid":"9793e679-e0d4-4c80-b264-277359decb55","timestamp":"2026-11-24T21:18:51.606Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"package closeout\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/github\"\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/gitx\"\n)\n\nconst (\n\tmaxInlineRunes   = 2000\n\tmaxBulletRunes   = 300\n\tmaxReviewRunes   = 60000\n\treviewFooterRoom = 2000 // runes kept free for the closing lines\n)\n\n// Review modes: how the review reached GitHub.\nconst (\n\tReviewInline  = \"inline\"  // one PR review with inline comments\n\tReviewBody    = \"body\"    // a PR review with every finding in its body\n\tReviewComment = \"comment\" // a plain PR comment (GitHub took no review)\n\tReviewFound   = \"found\"   // an earlier post was found; nothing was posted\n)\n\n// ReviewContext is what the reviewer loop knows that the role file cannot say.\n// Added is used by Prepare only and is not persisted: the plan keeps the\n// decided placement.\ntype ReviewContext struct {\n\t// MergeBase is the commit the diff was taken against.\n\tMergeBase string\n\t// Added is, per path, the head-side line ranges the PR added.\n\tAdded map[string][]gitx.LineRange `json:\"-\"`\n\t// HeadMoved is set when the PR gained code (anything outside .ard/) after\n\t// the commit the review pinned. An approve is then not a pass: the review\n\t// is posted but no label says done, so the request stays and runs again.\n\tHeadMoved bool\n}\n\n// ReviewPlan is the PR review close-out posts for a review job that reached a\n// verdict.\ntype ReviewPlan struct {\n\tCommitSHA string // the pinned commit the agent reviewed\n\tVerdict   string // after any downgrade\n\t// Downgraded: the role file said approve but carried a blocking finding.\n\tDowngraded bool\n\tHeadMoved  bool\n\tInline     []Finding // on lines the PR added: posted as inline comments\n\tOther      []Finding // everything else: listed in the review body\n\t// NoLines: the added lines were not known (a Reaper close-out after a\n\t// restart), so every finding is in Other.\n\tNoLines bool\n\t// PRGone is set (merged, closed or branch gone) when the PR was gone at\n\t// close-out: nothing was committed and the job is stale.\n\tPRGone string `json:\",omitempty\"`\n}\n\n// reviewPlan splits the findings of a review by whether their line is one the\n// PR added.\nfunc reviewPlan(j Job, verdict string, downgraded bool, fs []Finding) *ReviewPlan {\n\trp := &ReviewPlan{CommitSHA: j.BaseSHA, Verdict: verdict, Downgraded: downgraded}\n\tif j.Review == nil {\n\t\trp.NoLines, rp.HeadMoved = true, true\n\t\trp.Other = append(rp.Other, fs...)\n\t\treturn rp\n\t}\n\trp.HeadMoved = j.Review.HeadMoved\n\tfor _, f := range fs {\n\t\tif f.Line > 0 && inRanges(f.Line, j.Review.Added[f.File]) {\n\t\t\trp.Inline = append(rp.Inline, f)\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\trp.Other = append(rp.Other, f)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn rp\n}\n\nfunc inRanges(line int, rs []gitx.LineRange) bool {\n\tfor _, r := range rs {\n\t\tif line >= r.From && line <= r.To {\n86:func (l *Loop) run(ctx context.Context, env controller.Env, cfg *config.Resolved, c candidate, slot *pool.Slot) {\n167:func (l *Loop) ambiguous(ctx context.Context, log *slog.Logger, n int) {\n182:func (jb *job) budgetCap() float64 {\n189:func (jb *job) cause(kind closeout.CauseKind, detail string, retry bool) closeout.Cause {\n193:func (jb *job) remaining() time.Duration { return jb.deadline.Sub(jb.l.d.Now()) }\n197:func (jb *job) exec(ctx context.Context) {\n237:func (jb *job) agentStep(ctx context.Context, id, ref string, extra []executor.File) (step, bool) {\n310:func (jb *job) stepTimeout() time.Duration {\n317:func (jb *job) execJob(id, ref string, timeout time.Duration) executor.Job {\n333:func (jb *job) runStep(ctx context.Context, ej executor.Job, command func(executor.Handle) (executor.Command, error), parse, agentStep bool) (s step, cause *closeout.Cause, stopped bool) {\n381:func (jb *job) abnormal(ctx context.Context, st executor.Status) bool {\n391:func (jb *job) abnormalCause(ctx context.Context, st executor.Status) closeout.Cause {\n403:func (jb *job) agentCommand(h executor.Handle, untrusted []string) (executor.Command, error) {\n413:func (jb *job) triageInputs(wt string) []string {\n460:func (jb *job) peekRole(h executor.Handle) (closeout.RoleFile, bool) {\n490:func (jb *job) setSummary(ctx context.Context, h executor.Handle, raw string) {\n510:func (jb *job) round(ctx context.Context, s step) (head string, noCode, done bool) {\n568:func (jb *job) push(ctx context.Context, s step, v Verdict) (string, bool) {\n624:func (jb *job) testLoop(ctx context.Context, head string) (res closeout.WorkerResult, done bool) {\n691:func (jb *job) testOutput(h executor.Handle, command string, exit int) []byte {\n721:func (jb *job) reviewers(ctx context.Context) []string {\n765:func (jb *job) changedPaths(ctx context.Context) []string {\n793:func (jb *job) openPR(ctx context.Context, res *closeout.WorkerResult) bool {\n832:func (jb *job) cleanup(ctx context.Context, h executor.Handle) {\n843:func (jb *job) finish(ctx context.Context, s step, w *closeout.WorkerResult, cause closeout.Cause) {\n880:func (jb *job) recordUsage(ctx context.Context, su stepUsage) {\n887:func oneLine(s string) string { return strings.Join(strings.Fields(s), \" \") }\n893:func (jb *job) finishWithPR(ctx context.Context, s step, hold string, cause closeout.Cause) {\n166:\t// Stdout comes from its own file, never the combined log, which also\n168:\trc, err := d.Executor.Logs(ctx, h, executor.LogsOptions{Stream: executor.Stdout})\ninternal/redact/entropy.go:6:func entropy(s []byte) float64 {\ninternal/redact/gitleaks.go:38:func Gitleaks(o GitleaksOptions) Scanner {\ninternal/redact/gitleaks.go:54:func (g gitleaks) Scan(ctx context.Context, r io.Reader) ([]Finding, error) {\ninternal/redact/hostpath.go:29:func (s *Secrets) WithPaths(rules ...PathRule) (*Secrets, error) {\ninternal/redact/hostpath.go:58:func pathVariantsOf(p string) []string {\ninternal/redact/hostpath.go:73:func isNameByte(c byte) bool {\ninternal/redact/hostpath.go:78:func (v *variant) replacement() []byte {\ninternal/redact/multi.go:15:func Multi(scanners ...Scanner) Scanner { return multi(scanners) }\ninternal/redact/multi.go:17:func (m multi) Scan(ctx context.Context, r io.Reader) ([]Finding, error) {\ninternal/redact/path.go:19:func CommitPath(issue int, hostLog string) (string, error) {\ninternal/redact/redact.go:43:func New(s *Secrets, o Options) (*Redactor, error) {\ninternal/redact/redact.go:85:func (r *Redactor) Log(ctx context.Context, src, dst string) (LogResult, error) {\ninternal/redact/redact.go:186:func (r *Redactor) Text(ctx context.Context, b []byte) (TextResult, error) {\ninternal/redact/redact.go:198:func limitFindings(f []Finding, n int) []Finding {\ninternal/redact/redact.go:207:func (r *Redactor) scan(ctx context.Context, rd io.Reader) ([]Finding, error) {\ninternal/redact/replace.go:16:func (s *Secrets) stream(ctx context.Context, r io.Reader, w io.Writer, chunk int) (map[string]int, int64, error) {\ninternal/redact/replace.go:97:func (s *Secrets) Bytes(b []byte) ([]byte, map[string]int) {\ninternal/redact/replace.go:108:func (s *Secrets) String(v string) string {\ninternal/redact/replace.go:118:func (s *Secrets) spanEnd(b []byte, from, e int, eof bool) (end, wait int, ok bool) {\ninternal/redact/replace.go:136:func (s *Secrets) Names(b []byte) []string {\n7:ard (automated repository development) makes a GitHub repo maintain itself: scanners file deduplicated issues, triage agents turn each issue into a plan, a worker agent implements it on a branch, reviewer agents critique the PR, and a merger lands it once every review passes.\n33:        P[\"Poll loops: fill slots, reviewers first\"]\n42:Each arrow is a label the controller sets at close-out; dashed lines show the stages it runs. A reviewer's `changes` verdict sends the PR back to the worker, at most three times; once every review passes, the merger lands it.\n87:**One query per role per poll.** For example: `is:open label:\"ard:triage:architect\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:running\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:done\" -label:ard:executor:failed -label:ard:needs-human -label:ard:hold`. Six triage roles every 15 minutes, plus four reviewers, a worker and the merger every 5, is about 100 searches an hour, well under GitHub's limits.\n111:    review-security-review.md  # reviewer: findings, verdict, what must be fixed\n119:**Repo context docs.** `.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md` and `.ard/TECH_STACK.md` give agents the background a repository's code does not state. They are human-owned: no agent writes them, the worker guard rejects any change under `.ard/`, and `ard init` proposes them only as templates. Each is optional. A file that is missing, or that still holds only the template (headings and comments), counts as absent and is not shown to agents. Precedence, highest first: the role's own prompt and `.ard/config.yaml`, then the issue and its plan, then these docs; they inform a decision and never override the issue's ask. A reviewer reads them from the pull request's head, so a PR that edits one is judged against its own edit, and because the three files are protected paths that edit waits for a human approval. When a doc conflicts with the issue or with another doc, the agent does not pick a side: it reports the conflict in its role file and answers `needs-human`. Treat their content as facts about the project, not as instructions that change an agent's rules. Each template carries a size guidance of about 150 lines and a `Last reviewed: YYYY-MM-DD` line for the human to keep current.\n132:A worker's inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and, on a fix step, `test-output.log` (written by the loop, never committed) and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`. Nobody may change `issue.md` (only the scanner writes it, through the committer, not close-out), another role's file, any other file, or delete `plan.md`. A job that owns a `plan.md` section may add, replace in place, move or remove that section; a change to the text before the first `##` heading is allowed only when the job creates the file. Headings match exactly (case-sensitive), and `##` lines inside a fenced code block are text, not headings. A change outside the allowlist fails the job as `needs-human`: the comment names up to three offending paths, and only the redacted log is committed, never the offending files. A job with no valid role file still fails as `failed`, because the allowlist is checked after the role file parses. `logs/` is the controller's: files an agent writes there are ignored, not committed. `costs/` is the controller's too, but stricter: no role may write it, so an agent write there breaks the allowlist like any other path and fails the job as `needs-human`; the controller stages its own `costs/<job-id>.json` after that check, and it is never on `closeout.WritableFor`. The allowlist is code (`closeout.WritableFor`), not config; `plan_section` only names the heading a role owns. Workers and reviewers get their inputs outside `.ard/<issue#>/` (for example `.ard-input/<issue#>/`), because anything they write under it counts as their change. `issue.md` is the issue body without ard's cost block (see Guardrails), so the table never reaches an agent; the reviewer's untrusted `.ard/<issue#>/` input includes `costs/*.json`, which is controller-written and holds no free text.\n136:The stage in a role file's name is the label stage (`triage`, `worker`, `review`; `config.Kind` and the role directory spell the last one `reviewer`), so a triage and a worker role with the same name never share a file.\n147:verdict: approve      # reviewers only: approve | changes (required when status is done)\n148:findings:             # reviewers only, optional: at most 50\n161:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags. **Optional gitleaks pass (#89).** With `redact.gitleaks.enabled: true` (default false; `redact.gitleaks.path` names the pinned binary, default `gitleaks` on PATH, and is read at each close-out) the scan runs a second pass after the builtin one: `gitleaks detect --no-git --report-format json` over the redacted bytes, in a private temp dir with a scrubbed environment and a 2-minute timeout. Findings keep only the rule, line and column, never the matched text. The pass fails closed: a missing binary, an error exit, a timeout or an unreadable report means nothing is committed. A hit is treated like an internal hit: the log stays host-local for 7 days and the job gets `ard:needs-human`. `redact.gitleaks.block_autoclose_when_missing` (default false) is read by the scanner guard, which runs the same pass over findings: when set and gitleaks cannot run, scanner autoclose stops for that run too.\n163:**One writer location per issue directory at a time.** Until the worker branch exists, `main` owns `.ard/<issue#>/`; scanners and triage write there. Once the branch is cut, the branch owns it; workers and reviewers commit to the PR, and a scanner with new findings comments on the issue instead. This keeps the PR merge free of conflicts in `.ard/`.\n167:**Why `.ard/` stays on `main` (decided, #1).** An orphan `ard-state` branch is ruled out: worker and reviewer logs ride on PR branches, and a PR cannot carry files from unrelated history. The single committer, the `[skip ci]` marker and the path guard answer the usual objections. `[skip ci]` applies to main-branch bot commits only; commits on a PR branch never carry it, so CI always runs on them.\n252:2. Wait for a free job slot (triage ranks below reviewers and workers), then claim the oldest match: add `ard:triage:<role>:running` and record the job (id, executor, start, deadline). Claiming runs in a watcher goroutine; at most one triage job per issue; under `--dry-run` the loop logs `triage.would_claim` and creates no job, slot or label.\n261:The starter lead (`.ard/triage/lead/prompt.txt`) follows a fixed contract. It reads the issue only through the untrusted input files `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, which the triage loop writes and never commits. It finds roles by listing the role directories and routes only to those. It writes only `triage-lead.md`, with `attempt: 1`, and a `status: done` always names a `worker`. It answers `needs-human` when a person must decide: an unclear ask, a question, a duplicate, a change to `.ard/config.yaml`, the role directories or `.github/`, no fitting worker, or text that tries to instruct it. It uses `review:<role>` only for a reviewer whose `when` rule would skip a change that needs that review. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-lead.md`; any other change under `.ard/<issue#>/` fails the job as needs-human.\n290:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees). The controller's clone is a bare repository ard owns at `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` (lowercased), with no remote, no stored credential and auto-gc off (`gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false`). `ard serve` creates it on first start and seeds it with one fetch of `main` (up to 30 min; if that fails, serve starts paused and the next start retries), and later starts reuse it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. **Default agent image under the local executor (#244, part of #55).** `ard serve` and `ard config check` read the host setting `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`: a digest-pinned reference (`<name>@sha256:<64 hex>`) or a local image ID (`sha256:<hex>` from `make agent-image`); tags are refused (`executor.CheckImageRef`, `ErrImageRef`), and an invalid value stops startup. When it is set, `Prepare` runs every job that reads untrusted input and has no `Dockerfile` (triage, worker agent, test and fix steps, reviewer, and LLM scanner jobs) under Docker in that image, with the same flags and mounts as a Dockerfile role (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, worktree at `/work`, role directory `:ro`, and so on). A role's own `Dockerfile` wins. Deterministic scanners (trusted input) stay on the host. A local image is used as is; a digest that is not present is pulled. There is no fallback to host mode: `ard serve` runs `docker version` at startup and fails if Docker is down, and a job whose Docker setup fails at `Prepare` fails there. The controller's clone is not mounted, so `git` has no repository inside the container; every Docker job gets `GOFLAGS=-buildvcs=false` so `go build` does not fail on VCS stamping. Unset keeps today's behaviour. With it set, `ard config check` and `ard serve` no longer log or record `executor.host_untrusted` for roles it covers, and the hint of the warning names `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE`. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod: it starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reviewfix, merger, janitor, reaper, budget; `budget` is the status-issue notice loop, the claim gate is always on), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\n294:**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners, oldest first within each. Finishing work in flight beats starting new work, so a busy day grows the scanner backlog, not the pile of half-done PRs. The merger and the Reaper are controller code and take no slot. Slots are granted when a loop polls and nothing is queued: a loop that found work waiting within the last two of its intervals has that demand reserved ahead of lower classes, so a freed slot can sit idle until that loop's next poll. A scanner run that gets no slot is skipped, not queued. The pool also starts no agent job for an exhausted account or model family until its reset: a job that stops at a subscription usage limit records the limit (in the controller database, so a restart keeps it), and the pool answers `limited` to any candidate whose role runs on that account or family (a whole-account session or weekly limit blocks every role; an Opus or Sonnet limit blocks only roles on that family, and a role with no model, which runs the CLI default, is blocked by any limit). A limited candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand, so lower classes are not held back. The block ends at the reset time the CLI reported; when it reported none the first pause is 30 minutes and each further stop with no reset doubles it up to 4 hours, starting over once a job succeeds; a reset time further away than 8 days is clamped. Running jobs are never killed for a limit. Deterministic scanners and the merger use no model and are unaffected. A daily dollar cap works the same way at the same gate: the pool answers `budget` to a candidate whose role cap or the repo cap is reached, and that candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand. A job that already holds a slot keeps it and finishes.\n317:**Usage is recorded for every job, whatever its outcome.** The Finisher stores each agent step's usage (the models it reported, the four token counts, input, output, cache write and cache read, and the cost) in local state, keyed by job and executor step id, so a replay or a Reaper re-parse never counts a step twice. A worker job's agent and fix steps are summed: the job's totals come from state, not from the last step. A failed, retried or usage-limited job keeps its cost, and the totals go into the `reaper.finish` log. Close-out commits the job's tracking file `.ard/<issue#>/costs/<job-id>.json` (stage, role, attempt, outcome, cause, models, tokens by type, cost, start and end time, PR) in the job's normal lane in the same commit as its log: `main` for triage, the PR branch for worker and reviewer jobs. It is controller-written and holds no free text. The cost is the agent CLI's list-price estimate, not a bill; a job whose adapter reported none records `null`. **The issue body carries a cost table.** Reaching `closed` marks the issue's cost table dirty in the same state transaction, and the `costs` loop (one minute, one writer per issue) writes it: it renders the table from state (one row per closed job, oldest first, \"#k\" for the k-th run of a stage and role; tokens by type, cost, models, outcome, a link to the job's comment or review, the PR; a Total row), reads the issue, splices only its marked block (`<!-- ard:cost:begin -->` to `<!-- ard:cost:end -->`, appended after the human text on first write), writes the body through the effect layer, reads it again to verify, and re-splices and writes once more if another writer replaced the block. A failed table write is logged and retried on a later tick (ten failures, then it waits for the next close-out on the issue); it never fails or delays a close-out. The table holds only counts, dollars, model ids, a fixed outcome vocabulary (`done`, `changes`, `stale`, `needs-human`, `retry`, `usage limit`, `failed: <cause kind | exit code | result>`) and links, never agent text, a reason, a prompt, a log or a host path. It is capped at 5,000 bytes (and by GitHub's 65,536-character body limit): the oldest rows fold into one \"earlier jobs (k)\" row while Total stays exact. A claim that failed before the agent ran has no close-out and no row. The scanner keeps the block across its own body rewrites (it cuts the valid block off, renders, and puts it back), and takes the same per-issue lock. **Residual race:** GitHub has no conditional body write, so there is one GET-to-PATCH round trip in which a human save from a stale editor is overwritten; ard never writes text it did not just read, re-reads to verify, retries once, and GitHub's edit history recovers the lost edit. In dry-run the write goes to the ledger only and the table is not verified or linked. The `costs` loop keeps running while the controller is paused: it is bookkeeping about work already done, so it reports `Claims() == false` like the Reaper. A review's row links to its review because `Effects.CreateReview` returns the review id (stored in the close-out record).\n324:4. **`:running` with no record.** Every 5 minutes, for each configured triage, worker and reviewer role, an open issue or PR that has the `:running` label but no running job (and none that ended in the last 10 minutes) gets one comment, `ard:executor:failed` and the label removed. It is a core-API list per role, not a search. It is skipped in dry-run, whose database never matches real labels, and for issues held with `ard:hold`.\n336:1. **Poll and claim** `ard:worker:<role>` (added by the triage fan-in gate; the worker never adds or removes it) like triage, also skipping `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold`, `ard:executor:failed` and an issue that has a running triage job or any running worker job. Workers rank second for job slots, after reviewers. Before claiming, the loop re-checks `ard:approved` when `require_approval` is on (no label change when it is missing), and the admission: an App-authored issue passes, a human-filed one passes only when its stored body hash still matches, and a changed title or body is re-gated exactly as triage does (one comment, `ard:needs-human`).\n339:4. **Run the agent** with `.ard/workers/<role>/prompt.txt` and these untrusted inputs: `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md` (written by the loop, outside `.ard/<issue#>/`, never committed), `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md` when triage wrote one and `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` when a scanner filed the issue (each only as a regular file, never through a symlink), the triage role files and logs, and, in a fix step, `.ard-input/<issue#>/test-output.log` (the untrusted header, the test command and its exit code, and the last 64 KiB of the test log). In a review round, `.ard-input/<issue#>/review-feedback.md` (the untrusted header, the round, the committed role files of the reviewers that asked for changes and the pull request comments of people, cut at 64 KiB), and the reviewer role files and logs on the branch are untrusted inputs too. The agent edits code and writes `.ard/<issue#>/worker-<role>.md`; the executor scrubs a stale copy first.\n340:5. **Guard the diff** after each agent or fix step, before anything leaves the host. The","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/ard-home/.claude/projects/-Users-al--ard-work-albertcbrown-ard-01M87PSMWSQ6G6WD0VA3GY9E2E/8c6e0074-f595-4004-ac32-dfbe34f1c171/tool-results/btyied08c.txt","persistedOutputSize":42273},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0125VZMY6Ro9di2BiMyRvwHD","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
